The five together hold about 47% of 12,559 attributed lines.

**Caveats for picking maintainers**
- **Marcus Lindqvist's row is a guess.** I merged three addresses by name and address pattern only, and nothing was written to `.mailmap`. Unmerged, the addresses hold 546, 281 and 64 lines (4.3%, 2.2%, 0.5%). The top-five order doesn't change, but the 7.1% figure depends on the guess. Please confirm they are one person.
- **Only Marcus is recently active.** The other four last committed in 2015–2018. Noah Dietz and Jonathan Reyna have 1–2 commits each, so their lines come from a few large changes.
- **Copy detection moves some shares.** Without `-C`, Alex Ogier drops from 8.4% (1,053 lines) to 6.0% (747 lines). Riobard rises from 3.3% to 5.7% (708 lines), which would put Riobard roughly level with Ogier. With the default `-C`, Riobard has 412 lines (3.3%).
- **Next in line:** Tamal Saha (524 lines, 4.2%), then Mitch Connors (429 lines, 3.4%, last commit 2019). Mitch Connors appears as `therealmitchconnors` and is counted once because both names share one address.
- **Tail and concentration:** 87 more authors hold 16.4% together; the largest is Hu Jun at 0.9%. The line-based bus factor is 6, meaning six authors reach 50% of the lines. Reaching 80% takes 19 authors. Both are for the whole repository's Go code.

**What was left out**
- **Vendored code:** 1 file with 802 lines is excluded from every number.
- **Bots:** `github-actions[bot]` (77 lines, 1 commit) is excluded and gets no credit.
- **Mechanical rewrites checked:** I set aside `d43be5f`, the `go fix ./...` commit by Dana Whitfield that rewrote `interface{}` to `any` (38 surviving lines), and those lines went back to the earlier authors. I kept three commits that look mechanical but are small: `d16d05e` (bogem, 16 lines), `6404d82` (Tomas Aschan, 3 lines) and `92abf19` (bogem, 1 line).
- **Other duplicate names:** bogem (164 lines) is the same address as Albert Nigmatzianov. Charlie R.C (177 lines) is the same address as xilabao. These are not in the top five either way.

Line counts show whose code survived. They don't show review work, design or code that was deleted on purpose.
